    -->

    <!--
        ! Assignment 2.09: leave this commented until you reach 2.09.
        ! Then make two additions to the form above.

        1. tag_ids, on a line of its own directly above the first <group>.
           many2many_tags draws the links as coloured pills, and color_field
           says which field holds the palette index:

               <field name="tag_ids" widget="many2many_tags"
                      options="{'color_field': 'color'}"
                      placeholder="Tags..."/>

        2. a page titled "Compliance Checklist", after the Financials page,
           holding document_ids in an embedded editable list so paperwork can
           be added without leaving the loan:

               <page string="Compliance Checklist">
                   <field name="document_ids">
                       <list editable="bottom">
                           <field name="type_id"/>
                           <field name="name"/>
                           <field name="attachment_id"/>
                           <field name="state"/>
                       </list>
                   </field>
               </page>
    -->

    <!--
        ! Assignment 3.01: leave this commented until you reach 3.01.
        ! Then make two changes to the form above.

        1. The customer's contact details, straight under partner_id. They are
           related fields, read-only, filled in the moment a customer is
           picked:

               <field name="email"/>
               <field name="phone"/>

        2. The amounts in the Financials page. It replaces the loan_amount and
           down_payment lines there; delete those then. principal_amount is
           typed in, loan_amount is worked out from it. widget="monetary"
           shows the currency symbol, and it needs the invisible currency_id
           in the same group, which is already there:

               <field name="principal_amount" widget="monetary"/>
               <field name="down_payment" widget="monetary"/>
               <field name="loan_amount" widget="monetary"/>
    -->

    <!--
        ! Assignment 3.03: leave this commented until you reach 3.03.
        ! Then make three changes to the form above.

        1. The workflow buttons, in the header before the statusbar. A
           type="object" button calls the Python method named in name= on the
           record in the form. invisible takes a Python expression over the
           record's fields, so each button only shows in the state it starts
           from. The first one is written out:

               <button name="action_submit" type="object" string="Submit"
                       class="btn-primary" invisible="state != 'draft'"/>

           Add Approve (action_approve_loan) and Reject (action_reject_loan)
           the same way, both visible only while state is 'sent'. Give Approve
           class="btn-primary" and Reject class="btn-secondary".

        2. The decision dates, in the second column under date_applied, each
           hidden until it has a value:

               <field name="date_approved" invisible="not date_approved"/>
               <field name="date_rejected" invisible="not date_rejected"/>

        3. Approve and reject buttons on every checklist line, after the state
           column of the document_ids list. An icon button has no text, so it
           needs a title for the tooltip and for screen readers:

               <button name="action_approve_document" type="object"
                       icon="fa-check" title="Approve"/>
               <button name="action_reject_document" type="object"
                       icon="fa-times" title="Reject"/>

        Do not use attrs="{'invisible': ...}" from older Odoo versions. It
        was removed, and the view will not load with it.
    -->

    <!--
        ! Assignment 4.02: leave this commented until you reach 4.02.
        ! Then add the chatter to the form above, straight after </sheet> and
        ! still inside </form>. Inside the sheet it would be squeezed into the
        ! form's column; outside, Odoo gives it its own panel:

               <chatter/>
    -->

    <!--
        TODO (assignment 2.08): the search view.

        id loan_application_view_search, on loan.application, wrapping a
        <search> element that holds three kinds of child.

        1. Searchable fields. These are what the box at the top matches when
           someone types:

               <field name="name"/>
               <field name="partner_id"/>

        2. Filters, one per shortcut. Each needs a name, a label, and a domain:

               <filter name="filter_draft" string="Draft"
                       domain="[('state', '=', 'draft')]"/>

           Add one per state: Draft, Sent, Approved and Rejected.

           Then "My Applications", the filter every Odoo app has on records
           that belong to someone. uid is the logged-in user's id, available
           in any domain written in a view:

               domain="[('user_id', '=', uid)]"

           And Archived. It filters on active rather than state, and the value
           is the Python False, not the text "false":

               domain="[('active', '=', False)]"

           Put a <separator/> between each kind of filter. Filters in the same
           block are combined with OR, and a separator starts a new block,
           combined with AND. Without them, selecting Draft and My
           Applications would show records that are either, rather than both.

        3. Group By options, which set a context instead of a domain. Wrap
           them in a plain group so they land in their own column of the
           dropdown:

               <group>
                   <filter name="group_by_state" string="Status"
                           context="{'group_by': 'state'}"/>
               </group>

           Add one for state, one for user_id (Salesperson) and one for
           partner_id (Customer). Give every filter its own name: duplicates
           confuse Odoo about which one is active.
    -->

    <!--
        TODO (assignment 3.04): the "Assign to Me" server action.

        id loan_application_action_assign_to_me, model ir.actions.server,
        with these fields:

            name                "Assign to Me", the label in the Actions menu
            model_id            ref="model_loan_application", the model the
                                code runs on
            binding_model_id    ref="model_loan_application" again. This is
                                what puts the action in the Actions menu at
                                all; without it the action exists but nothing
                                shows it.
            binding_view_types  list, so it appears when records are selected
                                in the list view and not on the form
            state               code, meaning the action runs Python
            code                records.action_assign_to_me()

        records is the selection the user made, however many records that is.
        The method does the work, the action only calls it: logic kept in the
        model can be tested and reused, logic typed into a code field cannot.

        No view change is needed. Odoo adds an action with a binding_model_id
        to the Actions menu by itself.
    -->

</odoo>
